WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.840
Stack Up. Here's a high-tech, high-strategy juggling act that's loads of fun.

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:26.280
And welcome to NEStalgia, the only podcast playing every NES game in chronological order.

00:00:26.420 --> 00:00:27.720
I'm Mike. I'm Joe.

00:00:27.880 --> 00:00:29.360
I'm Sean. And I'm Sam.

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:31.740
And welcome to another episode of Nostalgia.

00:00:31.880 --> 00:00:33.960
Today, we're talking about Stack Up.

00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:35.500
Oh, no. Yeah.

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:37.340
Oh, no. How are we feeling?

00:00:37.820 --> 00:00:39.660
Well, the objective is in the name.

00:00:40.420 --> 00:00:41.660
To stack things up.

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:43.620
I never really thought about that.

00:00:43.820 --> 00:00:45.100
Yeah. No, of course I thought about that.

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:48.460
It's not as ominous as Gyromite.

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:51.780
Right. Yeah. Well, it's also called Robot Block, apparently.

00:00:52.120 --> 00:00:55.200
Just like Gyromite. You brought that up, and I just want to talk about this real fast.

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:58.840
The reason why those things have those names is because they had to ship these things out

00:00:58.840 --> 00:01:02.900
so fast that they just wound up using the Famicom carts in them.

00:01:02.900 --> 00:01:12.380
So what's funny is it actually backfired for Nintendo in a way because these, for the longest time, were like a homebrew kind of way to play Famicom games.

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:14.920
Like you would, because it had the loader inside of it.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:23.000
So if you could just put the, like whatever Famicom game you wanted inside that NES cart and plop it down, it would be able to read that Famicom cart.

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:26.320
And I think that's the only reason that it has any relevance whatsoever today.

00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:29.720
And I think it's also one of the reasons why those games still fetch some kind of value.

00:01:29.900 --> 00:01:36.760
It's interesting if they were going to use the Famicom cart that they wouldn't just name it Robot Block so it's consistent as to what's on the title of it.

00:01:36.760 --> 00:01:37.840
Well, Robot Block sounds very foreign.

00:01:38.060 --> 00:01:43.480
You know, I think like one thing they were trying to do with these games, specifically, and this is good for a Rob recap right here,

00:01:43.820 --> 00:01:46.800
Rob was sold, you know, as like an American accessory.

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:52.040
Like basically Toys R Us and other retailers were just like, no, we're not taking on any more video game consoles.

00:01:52.040 --> 00:01:56.440
So they were like, oh, this thing's also like an entertainment system and it has toys and look at this thing.

00:01:56.680 --> 00:01:58.560
So they were able to like sell it that way.

00:01:58.780 --> 00:02:05.400
But Gyromite still sounds pretty foreign to me, but I think Stack Up is like a more, you know, it's an English American sounding thing.

00:02:05.720 --> 00:02:07.440
It makes me think of stacking up some burgers.

00:02:08.540 --> 00:02:09.400
Yeah? Yeah.

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:14.660
Just good old American pastime of just stacking up some burgers on the grill.

00:02:14.900 --> 00:02:15.880
Yeah, exactly. Yeah.

00:02:16.040 --> 00:02:17.940
It makes me want to buy toys and stack burgers.

00:02:17.940 --> 00:02:19.980
Are you saying Stack Up's like a sequel to Burger Time?

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:21.480
Yes. Great.

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:31.660
Yeah. So let's get a little bit into this.

00:02:31.900 --> 00:02:36.280
So Stack Up is one of the two games that came with Rob, or at least.

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:37.400
Didn't come with Rob.

00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:38.620
Interestingly enough. Was for Rob.

00:02:38.840 --> 00:02:40.000
Yeah. This one's for Rob.

00:02:40.100 --> 00:02:43.920
But interestingly enough, this one came with like the pieces too for Stack Up.

00:02:44.020 --> 00:02:45.500
Like they didn't come with Rob himself.

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:51.100
So this game fetches a pretty big value if it's complete, because it's the only way you're going to get all those little plastic pieces.

00:02:51.100 --> 00:02:52.600
If for some reason you want them.

00:02:52.720 --> 00:02:56.380
Did this game go for standard NES price when it came out?

00:02:56.460 --> 00:02:57.720
When it came out? That's a good question.

00:02:57.920 --> 00:03:01.760
Yeah. I imagine it was probably like one of those things where it's like standard price plus $10 or something like that.

00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:03.160
But these are like little plastic pieces.

00:03:03.300 --> 00:03:05.740
Was there a standard price for the card? Well, there wasn't, but there was.

00:03:05.880 --> 00:03:12.560
So I think the generally agreed price is that these things were around like $35, $40 when they came out first.

00:03:12.560 --> 00:03:15.440
But some people have also said they went as high as $60 back then.

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:22.240
And I know that like even in the Super Nintendo days, sometimes it was like the length or quality of a game also meant how much it was going to cost.

00:03:22.420 --> 00:03:25.040
Like I remember Final Fantasy VI being like $70.

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:27.240
Yeah. And I was just like, this is a long game.

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:33.380
Mm-hmm. So we've got Gyromite, which is a game that you play with Rob.

00:03:33.640 --> 00:03:35.240
Yes. He's your wingman.

00:03:36.040 --> 00:03:42.460
But then now we have Stack Up, in which you're not playing anything with Rob.

00:03:42.680 --> 00:03:46.880
You're using this game as just software to control Rob.

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:49.260
It's almost, yeah, you're like mind controlling Rob.

00:03:49.260 --> 00:03:58.140
Right, well the games aren't really, I mean the games are like suggested in kind of in the programming, but they're not, they don't recognize that you've done anything in the game.

00:03:58.420 --> 00:04:07.500
Exactly, because like you needed Rob to play Gyromite, but there's nothing, Rob isn't interacting with Stack Up.

00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:08.860
He's not touching a controller this time.

00:04:08.900 --> 00:04:10.400
In the other one he was touching a controller.

00:04:10.600 --> 00:04:13.640
Yeah, this is just using the game to interface with Rob.

00:04:13.740 --> 00:04:14.640
So what is Rob doing?

00:04:15.860 --> 00:04:41.440
Essentially, he's picking up these, I mean, let me set this up a little more, there's five discs, all different very nice pretty colors, and there's five discs, and Rob is able to pick which one he wants to grab, whether it's the bottom most one or the top, grab that, and shift it amongst five other tiles that he has kind of floating around him, and then he drops down those discs, and it's not pretty, he had just like dropped them.

00:04:41.440 --> 00:04:45.120
You have to actually keep saying down, down, down, down, and then he's...

00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:46.760
And they say it in the game. Yeah, down.

00:04:46.760 --> 00:04:48.940
They just had some pretty good, uh...

00:04:48.940 --> 00:04:50.280
Some crazy, I was pretty, uh...

00:04:50.280 --> 00:04:53.700
Yeah, I was pretty surprised with that they were able to sample voices.

00:04:54.180 --> 00:04:59.160
I thought it was surprising when we saw that in Kung Fu, like, and this has a lot better voice emphasis over it.

00:04:59.160 --> 00:05:01.040
Yeah, it's probably like double the bit rate, I don't know.

00:05:01.040 --> 00:05:13.000
Yeah. Well, I heard, I heard the, uh, like, this is kind of a tangent, but when you boot up Sega, or when you boot up Sonic, the Sega, that thing, is like, apparently that's like a ton of the memory in the game.

00:05:13.180 --> 00:05:14.500
Yes. That voice.

00:05:14.720 --> 00:05:16.980
Yeah, just to get it to sound so crisp and clear too.

00:05:17.120 --> 00:05:31.620
Yeah. So, I've heard that too, and there's a whole thing too, uh, with Sonic, where basically one of the guys who worked on Sonic 3D Blast, basically one of the developers decided, I'm gonna make a whole YouTube channel where I just break down how I did Sonic 3D Blast.

00:05:31.680 --> 00:05:39.040
And it's a really good read, uh, I'll throw that, not read, watch, I'll throw that in the show notes for sure, because that's just interesting too about, since you were talking memory and stuff like that.

00:05:39.040 --> 00:05:48.000
Essentially, he takes a 3D game and shrinks the intro video, which is CG, down to like 512 kilobytes or something like that.

00:05:52.980 --> 00:05:55.600
Okay, so let's start talking about Stack Up here for a minute.

00:05:55.680 --> 00:05:57.660
Can we just do, like, first impressions really fast?

00:05:57.660 --> 00:06:03.260
I know we kind of jumped into it right away, but Joe, you want to take us through your initial impression upon booting up the game?

00:06:03.260 --> 00:06:18.600
I mean, I think the first thing, aside from, again, the name being different than what's on the cart, the first thing that I noticed was that, kind of what Sean was saying, is that you're not actually, the game is not actually, like, programmed to register if you're playing the game.

00:06:18.760 --> 00:06:24.700
Like, you're using the game to control Rob and, like, do what it says, but you don't have to do that.

00:06:24.740 --> 00:06:26.320
You can do whatever you want, and you can just say you won.

00:06:26.860 --> 00:06:36.120
Yeah, this game right now, I mean, I don't think any of us play it the correct way, because, again, just like back with Jyramite, we don't have Rob.

00:06:36.400 --> 00:06:37.540
And I don't really want him.

00:06:37.640 --> 00:06:40.500
I don't want Rob, after what I'm seeing with Stack Up.

00:06:40.560 --> 00:06:42.420
I mean, yeah, I've educated myself.

00:06:42.420 --> 00:06:48.420
I've watched videos of Rob doing this, like, doing the stacking of the up.

00:06:48.660 --> 00:06:51.440
Yeah, and I'll link to that in the show notes, too, because I think that's worthwhile.

00:06:51.440 --> 00:06:58.740
I think watching Rob in action just kind of shows how something so cool can look so poor, can actually perform so poor.

00:06:59.300 --> 00:07:07.420
Exactly. So, yeah, no, it's fascinating just to watch, like, this was a thing that was a selling point for a system at some point.

00:07:07.780 --> 00:07:10.780
And, I mean, robots were big in the 80s and stuff like that.

00:07:11.100 --> 00:07:20.860
Of course. I think just, like, coming off of Star Wars and, you know, Star Trek on TV and stuff like that, it's like the idea of, you know, those droids and then, like, you know, now robots and stuff like that.

00:07:20.860 --> 00:07:21.960
It's like, I think that was huge.

00:07:22.100 --> 00:07:25.660
It was kind of like a reawakening era from, like, the 50s kind of sci-fi.

00:07:25.740 --> 00:07:26.900
Now it's back in the 80s.

00:07:27.020 --> 00:07:29.440
So I thought Rob is definitely an interesting thing to sell.

00:07:29.520 --> 00:07:32.460
It's just that when you see him actually do stuff, it's boring.

00:07:32.820 --> 00:07:43.740
So the unfortunate thing is that we can't actually give a first impression on our firsthand account with this game because there's no way for us to truly play the game.

00:07:44.120 --> 00:07:49.340
Yeah. I mean, my first impression was that I thought the music was really great when I booted it up.

00:07:49.600 --> 00:07:51.140
You know, I think that's one of the strong points.

00:07:51.280 --> 00:08:02.940
But then I kind of jumped into the game and every time you land on something, your screen kind of, like, it kind of shifts green for a frame or something like that.

00:08:03.120 --> 00:08:06.980
It's giving instructions to Rob and I thought something was wrong with it at first.

00:08:07.480 --> 00:08:09.180
So that was kind of my first impression.

00:08:09.180 --> 00:08:10.680
I thought that it was broken.

00:08:10.680 --> 00:08:17.420
It's the same mode of communication that was used in Gyromite, but unfortunately Sam wasn't with us for that game.

00:08:20.260 --> 00:08:29.340
Yeah, it is a little different from Gyromite in the sense that you're using the controller to control someone on screen to use, like, a digital controller.

00:08:29.880 --> 00:08:32.820
And I was just thinking, like, why have that middleman?

00:08:33.000 --> 00:08:37.260
Well, if you look at the controller, it's got something like nine buttons.

00:08:37.720 --> 00:08:40.820
Well, no, it has open, close, up, down, left, right.

00:08:41.080 --> 00:08:41.920
Up, down, left, right, A, B.

00:08:42.000 --> 00:08:43.280
You can use the same thing. Oh, you're right.

00:08:43.460 --> 00:08:45.180
It's true. And start for the end.

00:08:45.540 --> 00:08:54.400
Right. And my only thought was that if you are just controlling Rob, it feels like you're just controlling Rob.

00:08:54.400 --> 00:09:02.860
But if you're controlling this character and he's giving Rob these commands, it feels like, oh, there's a partnership between Rob and this character.

00:09:02.960 --> 00:09:08.000
I mean, as kind of silly as that is, I mean, that's the only thought process I can think of that would make them do that.

00:09:08.100 --> 00:09:15.920
Yeah. And so I think what's weird here is that you have, you know, essentially in this game, like we said, you're moving these disks across different patterns that the game is telling you.

00:09:15.920 --> 00:09:23.000
So the game is basically saying out of these five blocks, take the three blocks on the top and move them to column two if you're in column three or something like that.

00:09:23.040 --> 00:09:26.500
So you basically just have to hop around on the input screen to give those codes.

00:09:26.600 --> 00:09:30.140
I think what Joe is getting at to here is why have the NES at all?

00:09:30.260 --> 00:09:37.860
Why not Rob just have a controller that's hooked up to him and you just have those buttons exactly how they look like it's like a nine dial thing or whatever.

00:09:37.860 --> 00:09:40.320
And you just push those buttons and Rob moves around.

00:09:40.600 --> 00:09:43.700
It's like, do we really need a screen that shows us this?

00:09:43.880 --> 00:09:46.000
It just seems I think it seems very rushed.

00:09:46.160 --> 00:09:51.320
I think that's kind of why there's that whole move the person on the screen element to it.

00:09:51.400 --> 00:09:53.180
It's like, look, no, it's a game.

00:09:53.380 --> 00:09:54.960
You see, you got to move the little guy.

00:09:55.080 --> 00:09:59.140
That's the game. And if you're going to play the game and there's a whole separate game with Rob, you know.

00:09:59.380 --> 00:10:02.920
Right. Well, this is also we're talking about one mode right now.

00:10:03.200 --> 00:10:04.500
This is direct mode, correct?

00:10:04.860 --> 00:10:07.600
But I don't feel like the other two modes.

00:10:07.740 --> 00:10:08.620
No, not direct mode.

00:10:08.740 --> 00:10:09.700
This is not direct mode.

00:10:10.120 --> 00:10:11.240
Yeah, this is direct, right?

00:10:11.320 --> 00:10:12.140
Oh, OK. It is direct.

00:10:12.240 --> 00:10:13.700
Yeah, sorry. I'm thinking of memory as well.

00:10:13.820 --> 00:10:14.940
But memory is kind of the same.

00:10:15.100 --> 00:10:19.800
You're still, you know, like you're not putting it in as a controller input, but you're still telling it what to do next.

00:10:20.340 --> 00:10:29.520
I guess the other thought process could be like, you know, they need the screen to tell you what they want, what your next goal is to have, you know, to change it.

00:10:29.580 --> 00:10:31.060
So this color is on top or to change it.

00:10:31.180 --> 00:10:32.360
So these blocks are over here.

00:10:32.360 --> 00:10:34.380
So the screen tells you the next thing you have to do.

00:10:34.500 --> 00:10:36.960
And then they probably thought, like, well, there's not enough happening on the screen.

00:10:37.140 --> 00:10:39.860
So they needed needed this guy jumping around on a controller.

00:10:40.400 --> 00:10:46.260
And the only way to get any satisfaction to get that Pavlovian response.

00:10:46.420 --> 00:10:50.340
If I did something is to you're on the honor system with this game.

00:10:50.720 --> 00:10:53.100
You have to tell it, I beat the level.

00:10:53.460 --> 00:10:54.420
So you press start.

00:10:54.640 --> 00:10:57.960
So you could you could just press start immediately and say, hey, I beat this level.

00:10:58.140 --> 00:11:05.180
Right. Yeah. And if you if you fail what and you and you and you are an honorable person and you don't press start, that's it.

00:11:05.280 --> 00:11:07.420
Right. You're just done. You have to press select to go back to.

00:11:07.600 --> 00:11:09.080
Yeah. You're like, oh, I screwed up.

00:11:09.080 --> 00:11:13.560
Yeah. So, I mean, I feel like most kids would probably just press start again and be like, yeah, I won.

00:11:13.880 --> 00:11:19.360
Yeah. I feel like it's also the kind of thing where your score, because it's on the honor system, your score kind of has less merit.

00:11:19.600 --> 00:11:23.680
And you can't show off your score to anyone because they won't believe you.

00:11:23.820 --> 00:11:26.420
You have to record yourself on VHS.

00:11:26.820 --> 00:11:32.600
Yeah. So the direct system, besides working on the honor system, it has a couple of other things going for it.

00:11:32.700 --> 00:11:37.500
I guess it has that par for the course kind of thing where it's like, hey, it should take this many steps to get to get things done.

00:11:37.500 --> 00:11:45.960
And that's like a cool way, I guess, in terms of like, instead of a high score, even though it gives you a score, it's more competitive, I guess, to say like, well, I should try and do this under this certain amount of steps.

00:11:46.200 --> 00:11:51.760
But I do think we just we can just gloss over that honor system just by saying that it exists.

00:11:51.820 --> 00:12:00.400
It completely breaks this game because to Joe's point, if you fail, yes, you could be honorable and hit select and be like, I'm just going to start over again.

00:12:00.540 --> 00:12:03.720
But it just kind of like it breaks up the whole pattern of the game.

00:12:03.720 --> 00:12:10.400
When you move from phase one to phase two, it takes the pattern that you made in phase one and then says, OK, now change it into this.

00:12:10.520 --> 00:12:19.800
So if you move three of the top disks over to column two, the next phase is going to say, OK, now take like one of those disks from column two and put it in column five.

00:12:19.940 --> 00:12:26.160
But if you didn't do that correctly, it's like now you have to reset up Rob all over again just to put in inputs from the first phase.

00:12:26.160 --> 00:12:35.320
It's a little flawed in even the terms of like if you don't go by the honor system, if you just mess up, it's basically like a harder reset than just hitting the reset button on the NES.

00:12:35.440 --> 00:12:40.020
At least if I screw up in like Kung Fu, I just hit the reset button and start the whole game over again.

00:12:40.280 --> 00:12:42.480
This is like now I got to set everything back up.

00:12:42.600 --> 00:12:43.640
I got to do this.

00:12:43.740 --> 00:12:45.760
I got to it's too much trouble for what it's paying.

00:12:45.760 --> 00:12:52.000
Exactly. I think it's really testing the attention span of the kids that this is marketed towards as well.

00:12:52.520 --> 00:12:53.660
Yeah, that's a great point.

00:12:53.840 --> 00:12:57.520
And I think like, sure, this is this is early in the NES's lifespan.

00:12:57.520 --> 00:12:59.240
And again, it's another Rob game.

00:12:59.240 --> 00:13:01.120
So we weren't expecting much.

00:13:01.460 --> 00:13:05.920
It's kind of sad when you look at Rob and you see that this is these are the two games and that's all he has.

00:13:06.080 --> 00:13:07.480
And it's just like to see these two games.

00:13:07.640 --> 00:13:09.040
It's like, what else could they have done?

00:13:09.480 --> 00:13:10.340
I'm not asking that.

00:13:10.340 --> 00:13:19.980
I'm not asking for any designers here because I think Rob, just based on his functionality of turning left, turning right, picking up things like there wasn't much else he could have done.

00:13:20.100 --> 00:13:24.860
Yeah, I think also just the fact that Rob doesn't communicate back to the NES really limits you.

00:13:25.060 --> 00:13:30.400
I mean, I think there would be a lot of games that would have this honor system if they were to do other games with with Rob.

00:13:41.280 --> 00:13:43.520
All right. So that's pretty much the direct mode.

00:13:43.700 --> 00:14:00.880
Let's talk a little bit about memory, which I think if you're playing with Rob correctly and you are an honorable person, this offers something a little better than the direct mode because it's kind of like a mixture of stack up and Simon says or the Simon, the Simon game where you have to memorize your patterns.

00:14:00.880 --> 00:14:19.200
It kind of feels a bit like, I don't know if you guys were ever in, you ever took like an engineering class, but like there was a CNC machine and you have to give very specific instructions of what this machine is going to do for it to, it would basically like carve a shape in some plastic.

00:14:19.200 --> 00:14:25.580
So you had to give it instructions of like move it this amount of millimeters up and then to the left and then to the right.

00:14:25.760 --> 00:14:29.180
So it had it almost feels like very basic programming.

00:14:29.440 --> 00:14:36.800
Right. Because you're just writing the code per se and then it is is executing that once you press end.

00:14:36.940 --> 00:14:38.140
That's an interesting way to look at it.

00:14:38.240 --> 00:14:44.480
Yeah. Because it's definitely like testing your ability to see to see so many steps ahead.

00:14:44.720 --> 00:14:46.600
Exactly. And still understand what your results are going to be.

00:14:46.600 --> 00:14:53.840
But and so I think, you know, a lot of people might be wondering, well, how would you play a game like memory if you're not playing with Rob?

00:14:54.220 --> 00:14:55.960
And the answer is it's very easy.

00:14:56.080 --> 00:15:03.120
It's actually insane that all you have to do is just you have a sheet of paper or even like some graph paper and just sketch it out.

00:15:03.260 --> 00:15:04.100
And that's what I did.

00:15:04.200 --> 00:15:05.900
I just was sketching out each turn and seeing.

00:15:06.060 --> 00:15:10.180
And I mean, that sounds pretty tedious, but I don't see how that's less tedious than just watching Rob do it.

00:15:10.280 --> 00:15:11.540
I did that, too, actually.

00:15:11.700 --> 00:15:12.600
I wrote I wrote it out.

00:15:12.600 --> 00:15:18.720
And that kind of made me think that, like, you know, Rob kind of just gets in the way of what could be like an interesting puzzle game.

00:15:19.120 --> 00:15:20.820
But here's the thing, though. You wrote it all out.

00:15:20.940 --> 00:15:22.720
You didn't need to. You didn't even need the game.

00:15:23.340 --> 00:15:25.860
Yeah. You didn't write it all out right now in the studio.

00:15:26.120 --> 00:15:28.340
Right. The game is what tells you your objective.

00:15:28.560 --> 00:15:29.860
No, but the game tells me.

00:15:29.980 --> 00:15:36.540
But I could just have my friend come over and say, hey, can you just make it so that, you know, the five that you have in the middle, can you make them so that two go over to the left?

00:15:36.540 --> 00:15:38.240
That's the case with a lot of puzzle games.

00:15:38.460 --> 00:15:40.680
If you can come up, you can, you know, do something similar.

00:15:40.820 --> 00:15:48.360
You have someone. OK, but I beg to differ a little bit on that just because a future game will be talking about Wrecking Crew.

00:15:48.640 --> 00:15:54.940
I don't think I could just make up a Wrecking Crew puzzle on the fly on a piece of paper and then say solve it.

00:15:55.040 --> 00:15:57.360
And you would be able to, you know, like that requires inputs and movement.

00:15:57.760 --> 00:15:58.840
This doesn't really require that.

00:15:58.840 --> 00:16:08.720
This requires me to say, OK, down, down, grab, up, left, left, down, down, down, down.

00:16:08.900 --> 00:16:12.180
So I think you need Rob to be there for this to mean anything.

00:16:12.760 --> 00:16:14.640
Right. And of course you can solve it.

00:16:14.680 --> 00:16:23.880
But even with Rob there, I feel like it's just more, it would be more fun if those were on the screen and I could just see the input happen and it could tell me I won and tell me I lost.

00:16:36.960 --> 00:16:43.360
And then there's this other weird mode, which I can only imagine is trying to get catered to that American audience of bingo.

00:16:44.180 --> 00:16:45.900
But it's really not bingo at all.

00:16:46.040 --> 00:16:51.820
I actually thought I was going to be playing like a game of bingo with Rob and I should have read the manual.

00:16:52.080 --> 00:16:55.060
But boy, was it just direct mode again.

00:16:55.280 --> 00:16:57.980
Only now the inputs are five times as tedious.

00:16:57.980 --> 00:17:00.880
This is the closest we get to a video game now.

00:17:01.160 --> 00:17:02.720
Because there's enemies on the screen?

00:17:02.920 --> 00:17:07.380
Because anything that you're doing there, at least the game has an effect on it.

00:17:07.780 --> 00:17:12.780
Everything else that we're doing with this game is just using it as a controller.

00:17:13.040 --> 00:17:15.560
Right. It's almost like playing with a calculator in all the other games.

00:17:15.660 --> 00:17:21.800
And now it's like, imagine if your calculator had two things on it that were also hitting the C button to delete all your work.

00:17:22.680 --> 00:17:25.760
Well, so this one is the only one that I thought could be interesting.

00:17:25.760 --> 00:17:29.500
I didn't get to play this way, but if you play two player, I mean, you're fighting.

00:17:29.700 --> 00:17:30.860
You're fighting over blocks.

00:17:31.220 --> 00:17:33.760
With Professor Vector. But you're fighting with a second player.

00:17:33.960 --> 00:17:37.260
Yeah, no, well, yeah. There's Professor Hector, who you always are.

00:17:37.360 --> 00:17:39.420
Sorry, if you're playing the one player mode, you're always Professor Hector.

00:17:39.640 --> 00:17:41.660
And then the two player guy is Professor Vector.

00:17:41.720 --> 00:17:43.280
And those are the same two guys from Gyromite.

00:17:43.400 --> 00:17:44.420
So that's the only reason why I know that.

00:17:44.420 --> 00:17:46.360
They're the only people that ever associate with Rob.

00:17:46.380 --> 00:17:47.620
How does the two player work then?

00:17:47.620 --> 00:17:51.060
So basically, the one player bingo, it's basically the one player bingo.

00:17:51.220 --> 00:17:52.920
There's two enemies on the screen that you have to avoid.

00:17:53.120 --> 00:17:59.260
And you have to create a vertical or a horizontal row to select up.

00:17:59.380 --> 00:18:01.240
You know, one row is up and one row is down.

00:18:02.120 --> 00:18:03.720
But then there's two players on there.

00:18:03.880 --> 00:18:08.760
One of them is trying to get all the blocks to one side while the other one is trying to get all the blocks to the other side.

00:18:08.780 --> 00:18:10.640
But you're both controlling the same Rob.

00:18:10.640 --> 00:18:15.660
So, I mean, I imagine this probably takes like hours if either of you are any good at it.

00:18:15.740 --> 00:18:19.700
Because every input, you're just competing for which direction he's going to go.

00:18:19.700 --> 00:18:21.440
I used to play a game like this with my brother.

00:18:21.960 --> 00:18:24.360
And it's actually just surprising how I just thought about this.

00:18:24.460 --> 00:18:26.320
But basically, my brother's only a year younger than me.

00:18:26.400 --> 00:18:28.860
But when you're very little, that makes all the difference in the world.

00:18:29.020 --> 00:18:30.260
So I think I was five.

00:18:30.360 --> 00:18:38.100
He was probably four. And we used to play this game that I came up with called Stack the Blocks in Color-Coded Order While I Knock Them Down.

00:18:38.100 --> 00:18:42.360
And essentially, my brother would go around the room, stack up these big, and they were pretty big blocks.

00:18:42.640 --> 00:18:43.900
Yellow, red, and blue.

00:18:44.040 --> 00:18:47.120
That was all the colors. But you had to take all the blocks around the room, stack them up.

00:18:47.220 --> 00:18:49.160
And I would just go around and kick them.

00:18:49.380 --> 00:18:53.500
And if he was able to get them all stacked up in Color-Coded Order before I knocked them all down.

00:18:53.500 --> 00:18:54.320
That's an unwinnable game.

00:18:54.500 --> 00:18:55.660
Right, an unwinnable game for sure.

00:18:55.760 --> 00:18:56.740
That sounds like a great game for you.

00:18:56.740 --> 00:18:58.560
Kind of sounds like bingo two-player.

00:18:58.920 --> 00:19:01.220
Exactly. It's unwinnable and unlosable.

00:19:01.320 --> 00:19:02.160
It just goes on forever.

00:19:02.520 --> 00:19:07.220
I never, you know, I didn't even think about how much Stack-Up applies to my life until this moment.

00:19:07.540 --> 00:19:13.780
Wow. But just to be real clear about what bingo mode is, just in case we weren't being clear enough.

00:19:13.980 --> 00:19:19.260
It is the direct mode, again, only instead of hitting up to, or down rather, you know,

00:19:19.280 --> 00:19:21.960
because you have to move Rob down, down, down to select the disks.

00:19:22.180 --> 00:19:27.420
You have to complete a whole row of five inputs just to get him to go down one.

00:19:27.920 --> 00:19:32.040
And then he'll, like, you know, he'll do that input on the actual Rob.

00:19:32.040 --> 00:19:38.280
All while there are these things jumping around the screen, canceling out the buttons you've already pressed.

00:19:38.420 --> 00:19:43.100
Right. And if you jump back over a button you pressed, you un-press it, so you've got to make sure your straight lines are always...

00:19:43.100 --> 00:19:48.640
It's un-mess. I'm actually glad that, I mean, like, I tested the mode, but I didn't play this one with pen and paper, because that would have been crazy.

00:19:48.860 --> 00:19:55.620
But I'm also glad I didn't have to, like, sit through, you know, 45 minutes of Rob doing this, too.

00:19:55.820 --> 00:19:57.840
And I mean, like, 45 minutes is an exaggeration.

00:19:57.840 --> 00:19:59.840
I don't think it's actually going to take you 45 minutes.

00:20:00.040 --> 00:20:02.200
I think it will feel like 45 minutes.

00:20:02.640 --> 00:20:06.780
And that's actually something we didn't talk about when we were talking about memory mode.

00:20:07.120 --> 00:20:12.720
Because when you, after you select your input, it takes you to a screen where you select the speed,

00:20:13.220 --> 00:20:16.880
which I'd imagine would always be the same speed that you need Rob to move.

00:20:17.260 --> 00:20:22.900
But that'd be something you'd have to kind of figure out, because if the input comes in too soon before Rob's finished...

00:20:22.900 --> 00:20:26.120
Maybe we really want to, like, just take it all in when he's doing it.

00:20:26.280 --> 00:20:27.640
Do it as slow as possible.

00:20:29.380 --> 00:20:30.800
Wow, it's so futuristic.

00:20:31.320 --> 00:20:41.100
Yeah. So, any stray thoughts?

